CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanisms are explicit, but their observable effect depends on runtime lifecycle paths, the database's CURRENT_CATALOG() response, existing bootstrap settings, catalog metadata availability, and whether catalog-selection operations fail.

Databricks now creates a specialized execution context that discovers, tracks, resolves, and propagates the active catalog. Fresh contexts query the database for CURRENT_CATALOG(), while contexts initialized from another context attempt to adopt its default catalog. Catalog-selection and discovery failures are logged rather than propagated in several paths.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When the Databricks data source creates an execution context, callers now receive a Databricks-specific context with active-catalog tracking rather than the generic execution-context implementation.
  • A newly initialized Databricks context refreshes its defaults by querying the connected database for its current catalog. A non-empty result becomes the context's tracked catalog and, when bootstrap settings are in use and contain no default catalog, is copied into those settings.
  • A Databricks context initialized from another context now attempts to select the source context's default catalog, so related contexts can begin in the same catalog when that catalog is available.
  • If changing the default catalog fails with DBCException, the new Databricks context logs the failure and returns normally instead of reporting the failure to its caller.

Investigate
  • Trace execution-context construction and initialization to determine which connection, metadata, editor, and auxiliary-context lifecycles use the new context and whether initFrom is always Databricks-specific.
  • Inspect catalog-selection semantics across the superclass, JDBC driver, and Databricks server, including whether CURRENT_CATALOG() is supported in all relevant modes and whether catalog objects are loaded before name-based resolution.
  • Determine the ownership and persistence semantics of bootstrap settings because refreshDefaults writes a server-observed catalog into that object when its default is empty.
  • Review callers' expectations for DBCException from setDefaultCatalog and whether continuing after a failed catalog switch can execute later work in an unintended catalog.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not show which execution-context lifecycle callers invoke createExecutionContext and initializeContextState, or how frequently they create fresh versus copied contexts.
  • No HTTP route or endpoint is shown, so no web-facing route can be associated with these execution-context changes from this evidence.
  • The actual value returned by CURRENT_CATALOG(), and whether that name exists in the data source's loaded catalog model, depends on the connected Databricks environment.
  • The supplied code does not establish how GenericExecutionContext.refreshDefaults and GenericExecutionContext.setDefaultCatalog manipulate JDBC session state or what guarantees they provide after failure.
  • It is not shown whether getBootstrapSettings returns context-local, shared, or persistently stored settings, so the lifetime and wider effect of setDefaultCatalogName cannot be established.

